    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d143">

                <pb facs="0337" n="295" sameAs="frus:frus1951v04p1/pg295"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 143</seg>
                </opener>
                <note rend="inline" type="source">751.13/1–1351: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in France
                        </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d143-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-01-15T19:00:00-05:00">January 15, 1951—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3732. For Amb. ReEmbtel 4032 Jan 13<anchor n="2" xml:id="d143-f2"/> Pres today
                    approved ur extending invitation to Pleven Jan 29–30 informal visit Wash.</p>
                <p>Regret inability offer choice dates but Jan 29–30 are only days within suggestion
                    last para reftel and after Eisenhower has reported.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d143-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>If convenient to you it wld be helpful to us if you could be here for visit.</p>
                <pb facs="0338" n="296" xml:id="pg296"/>
                <p>Wld appreciate further detailed info on subjects Pleven desires discuss. Submit
                    any items you believe Pres or Sec shld raise that are not on Pleven’s list. Who
                    will accompany him.</p>
                <p>View press speculation hope visit will not leak pending issuance coordinated
                    Wash–Paris communiqué which shld be done soonest. Therefore suggest you consult
                    Pleven and submit to Dept brief draft statement to effect that as PM has
                    indicated desire to discuss with Pres mutual problems Pres has invited Pleven
                    visit Wash Jan 29–30,<anchor n="4" xml:id="d143-f4"/> etc.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d143-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d143-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted by Godley and cleared with Lacy, Director of the Office of Philippine and
                    Southeast Asian Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d143-f2" n="2" xml:id="d143-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d143-f3" n="3" xml:id="d143-f3-content">
                <p>For documentation on Eisenhower’s tour of various NATO capitals in January 1951,
                    see <ref target="frus1951v03p1#pg_392">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >iii</hi>, Part 1, pp. 392</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d143-f4" n="4" xml:id="d143-f4-content">
                <p>In telegram 4083 from Paris, January 16, Ambassador Bruce reported that Pleven
                    had accepted “with great pleasure” the invitation to visit Washington between
                    January 29 and 30 and that detailed information on subjects he wished to discuss
                    with the President and other American officials would be sent to Washington as
                    soon as possible. (751.13/1–1651)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
